  "text": ".content  \nLet's start by defining the function $f(n)$ which represents the product of the terms you've gave us:\n\\begin{align*}\nf(n) &= (\\sin^2(1) + \\cos^2(2)) \\cdot (\\sin^2(2) + \\cos^2(4)) \\cdot (\\sin^2(4) + \\cos^2(8)) \\cdots (\\sin^2(2^n) + \\cos^2(2^{n+1}))\n\\end{align*}\n\nFrom the trigonometry properties and the squared sum rule, we can see that the sum of a term and its complement is always $1$. Therefore, for each term $a_i = \\sin^2(2^{i-1}) + \\cos^2(2^i)$, we can simplify it as:\n\\begin{align*}\nf(n) &= (\\sin^2(1) + \\cos^2(2)) \\cdot (\\sin^2(2) + \\cos^2(4)) \\cdot \\ldots \\cdot (\\sin^2(2^n) + \\cos^2(2^{n+1})) \\\\\n&= (1 - \\cos^2(1)) \\cdot (1 - \\cos^2(2)) \\cdot \\ldots \\cdot (1 - \\cos^2(2^n)) \\\\\n&= (1 - \\cos^2(1)) \\cdot (1 - \\cos^2(2)) \\cdot \\ldots \\cdot (1 - \\cos^2(2^n)) \\\\\n&\\ \\ \\ \\ \\text{reducing each term }\n\\end{align*}\n\nNow, we need to calculate $\\sin^2(1)$ for the first term:\n\\begin{align*}\n\\sin^2(1) &= (1)^2 - \\cos^2(2) \\\\\n&= 1 - \\cos^2(2)\n\\end{align*}\n\nWe can continue calculating this recursively for terms up to $2^n$. Continuously, we can define $f(n)$ more directly as:\n\\begin{align*}\nf(n) &= (1 - \\cos^2(1)) \\cdot (1 - \\cos^2(2)) \\cdot \\ldots \\cdot (1 - \\cos^2(2^n) \\\\\n&\\ \\ \\ \\ \\text{and so on until 2^{n+1} on the right side})\n\\end{align*}\n\nNow, for $n=0$, we start the product. For $n=1$\n\\begin{align*}\n\\sin^2(1) + \\cos^2(2) = 1 - 2\\cos(1)\\sin(2) + \\sin^2(1) + \\cos^2(2)\n\\end{align*}\n\nAnd thus it follows the pattern. For the general case, observe that:\n\\begin{align*}\n\\sin^2(1) + \\cos^2(2) = \\sin^2(1) + \\cos^2(2^{n+1})\n\\end{align*}\n\nIt is clear that $\\sin^2(1) < 1$ continuously for each cosine and sine argument across $n$.\n\nContinuing to calculate $\\sin^2(1) + \\cos^2(2)$ for $n+1$, we can verify through computations that $\\sin^2(1) + \\cos^2(2)$ doesn't reach $\\frac{1}{2}$ for positive values of $n$.\n\nTo meet the requirement of the question \\($\\sin^2(1) + \\cos^2(2) = \\frac{1}{2}\\)$, we must look to strict values of $n$. So, $f(n)=2^{-n}$ via continuous simplification and \\begin{align*}\n2^{-4} &= 2^{n} \\implies n = 4 \n\\end{align*}\n\nHence, the smallest positive integer $n$ is:\n\\begin{align*}\n\\boxed{4}\n\\end{align*}\n\n 🚫assistant\n🖊",
